falco feeds by sysdigFalco Feeds extends the power of Falco by giving open source-focused companies access to expert-written rules that are continuously updated as new threats are discovered.learn moreOn November 18, 2026, F5 published CVE records for five memory-safety bugs in NGINX, and the project shipped fixes the same day in NGINX 1.31.9 (mainline) and 1.30.7 (stable). Each bug allows an unauthenticated remote attacker to corrupt memory in an NGINX worker process, and all five carry a CVSS 4.0 score of 9.2. Xint, one of the teams that reported the bugs, published a write-up dubbing the set of vulnerabilities “NGINX2Root.” Xint also chained one of the NGINX bugs with a previously unknown Linux kernel bug in the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) to get root on the host, including from inside a Docker container running the official NGINX image.At the time of publication for this blog, there has been no public proof-of-concept (PoC) or reported in-the-wild exploitation. However, several teams found the HTTP/2 bugs independently, and both Xint and Wiz researchers say they’ve built working exploits that bypass address space layout randomization (ASLR). Also, the Linux kernel bug has not yet been assigned a CVE, nor has a fix been released. The Sysdig Threat Research Team (TRT) reviewed the disclosures and tested post-exploitation activity in a lab to help defenders understand who is exposed, what they can do before a fixed package reaches every host, and what runtime detection can see. This blog covers the five NGINX bugs, the kernel half of the chain, and what defenders can do now.TimelineAll dates are in UTC.

