Image by Cybernews

Key takeaways

01

The n0n ransomware gang listed Visa and JPMorgan Chase as alleged victims and set a 48-hour leak deadline.

02

Visa says the incident involved a third-party provider in Asia Pacific, not Visa systems or payment data.

03

JPMorgan Chase says its service outage came from a software bug and has no evidence of a cyberattack.

04

The new gang recently claimed Expedia, alleging it stole billions of traveler records.

05

On November 19th, the gang has leaked Visa and JPMorgan customer data affecting thousands.

Key Takeaways by nexos.ai, reviewed by Cybernews staff.

Visa and JPMorgan Chase have landed on the hit list of a ransomware gang, which is giving the financial giants just 48 hours to negotiate before dumping their stolen data online.
The newly emerged ransomware gang, going by the name n0n, has listed both companies among its victims, setting a 48-hour countdown before the data leak.
The names on its victim list raise obvious concerns. Both companies operate at the heart of the global financial system, handling vast amounts of financial and personal information.
According to the attacker's leak site, the stolen Visa data includes:

Complete member profiles with full names, personal email addresses and mobile numbers across Asia Pacific markets

Travel and dining bookings for premium cardmembers: hotels, restaurants, cities, dates and confirmation references

Campaign engagement histories for more than one and a half million cardmember interactions

n0n also listed the data, allegedly stolen from JPMorgan, that included:

Engagement data from a premium Reserve card program operated by a global bank, covering hundreds of thousands of marketing campaign interactions across tens of thousands of unique cardholder email addresses.

The program's member directory, containing membership categories and postal codes.

Marketing emails featuring the bank's branding in their subject lines and sender addresses.

However, the attackers has not posted any screenshots of the data they claim to have exfiltrated, so at this point it is not possible to independently verify these claims.

